# Monitoring & Observability

## Stack

- **kube-prometheus-stack** — Prometheus, Grafana, Alertmanager
- **Loki + Alloy** — logs from stdout
- **BullMQ metrics** — queue lag, failed/completed jobs
- **Alerts** — OOMKilled, pod restarts, deployment down, queue lag, failed jobs rate

See [stage-monitoring.md](../../docs/runbooks/stage-monitoring.md) and [monitoring-deploy.md](../../docs/runbooks/monitoring-deploy.md).

## Code Changes

When adding or changing code, consider observability:

### New workers / queues

- Export metrics via `prom-client`: `waiting`, `active`, `failed`, `completed`, duration histogram
- Labels — low cardinality only: `queue`, not `jobId`, not `userId`

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD
bull_queue_waiting_jobs{queue="import"}

// ❌ BAD — cardinality explosion
bull_job_failed_total{jobId="123", userId="456"}
```

### New errors / critical paths

- Log via `createLogger(label)` from `@seller/shared`
- Consider adding an alert if the error indicates a system-level failure

### API endpoints

- Access logs — already in place
- Heavy operations — consider adding metrics (duration, count)

## Cardinality Rules

**Never** use high-cardinality values as labels (Prometheus, Loki):

| Avoid as label | OK as JSON field / log content |
| -------------- | ------------------------------ |
| `userId`       | ✓                              |
| `jobId`        | ✓                              |
| `requestId`    | ✓                              |
| `queue`        | — (use as label, low cardinality) |
| `level`        | — (use as label)               |

High-cardinality labels cause Prometheus/Loki to consume excessive memory and slow down queries.
